   shared src/styles/story.css). Content/structure kept; visual direction
   via the stylesheet. */
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';
import '../../styles/story.css';
---

<Shell title="Walking the Talk — Basit Masood" description="Lead Product Design Consultant for the world's largest culture-only consulting firm. Six tools, 200 organizations, acquired by ZRG Partners." active="stories">

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════
     ACT I · HERO — Culture, made measurable.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sp2-hero">
  <div class="sp2-hero-inner">
    <p class="sp2-hero-eyebrow">Walking the Talk · Lead Product Design Consultant · 2023 – 2024</p>
    <h1 class="sp2-hero-primary">Culture, made measurable.</h1>
    <p class="sp2-hero-secondary" style="max-width:none">Six tools. 200 organizations.<br/>Acquired by ZRG.</p>
    <p class="sp2-hero-desc">Walking the Talk is the world's largest culture-only consulting firm — clients run from Visa and Vodafone to Telstra, Westpac, eBay, and Lilly. Their consulting practice was bottlenecked by the tools underneath it. I redesigned their core assessment software into self-serve products that Fortune 500s could use without a consultant on the call — and the firm was acquired by ZRG Partners on the back of that shift.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════
     ACT I · PSYCHOMETRIC MOMENT
     Radial chart — 6 archetypes × 30 behaviors
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sp2-video-moment wtt-psych-moment">
  <img src="/landing/media/wtt.jpg" alt="The Walking the Talk software — analytics, culture scorecards, and survey tools, redesigned" />
  <div class="sp2-video-caption">The Walking the Talk software stack, redesigned</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════
     ACT II · CONTEXT — the consulting bottleneck
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sp2-stakes">
  <div class="sp2-stakes-inner">
    <div class="sp2-stakes-layout">
      <div class="sp2-stakes-stats">
        <div class="sp2-stakes-hero-stat">
          <span class="val">200+</span>
          <span class="label">Organizations</span>
        </div>
        <div class="sp2-stakes-supporting">
          <div class="stat"><span class="val">200K</span><span class="label">Leaders assessed</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="val">40</span><span class="label">Countries</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="val">50K+</span><span class="label">Taylor DB</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="val">6</span><span class="label">Tools shipped</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sp2-stakes-context">
        <h2>Culture consulting was <span class="accent">analog</span>.<br/>Assessments took <span class="accent">weeks</span>.<br/>Results arrived on PDFs.</h2>
        <p>Walking the Talk measured culture for Visa, Vodafone, Telstra, Westpac, Tesco Bank, eBay, Experian, Syngenta, Takeda, and Lilly. Their consultants were the best in the world at what they did — and they were the bottleneck. Every assessment needed a consultant to run it, compile it, and deliver it.</p>
        <p>The firm wanted its practice to scale without its people scaling linearly with it. That meant building the software layer underneath — tools a CHRO could run herself, get results from in minutes, and use in the boardroom the same week.</p>
        <p><strong>Six tools shipped. Fortune 500s self-serving. Acquisition followed.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════
     ACT III · THE STORY — five chapters
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sp2-story">
  <div class="sp2-story-inner">
    <div class="sp2-story-head">
      <h2>The Story</h2>
      <p>From consulting-only assessments to a software suite Fortune 500s ran themselves.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 01 -->
    <article class="sp2-chapter">
      <div class="sp2-chapter-index">
        <span class="sp2-chapter-num">01</span>
        <span class="sp2-chapter-date">2023 · The brief</span>
      </div>
      <div class="sp2-chapter-body">
        <h3>The consultants were the bottleneck.</h3>
        <p>Walking the Talk had the methodology — the decade-old frameworks, the archetype taxonomy, the Psynet psychometric engine behind it. What they didn't have was a software layer that let clients <em>run</em> the methodology. Every assessment needed a consultant to set up, score, and present. The practice couldn't scale past the calendar of its senior people.</p>
        <p>The brief was simple and unforgiving: take the three tools the firm ran on, and make them work without a consultant in the room.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Chapter 02 -->
    <article class="sp2-chapter">
      <div class="sp2-chapter-index">
        <span class="sp2-chapter-num">02</span>
        <span class="sp2-chapter-date">January 2024 · Taylor Assessment</span>
      </div>
      <div class="sp2-chapter-body">
        <h3>The only psychometric in the world that measures culture contribution.</h3>
        <p>Taylor Assessment scores individual culture contribution across <strong>six archetypes and thirty behaviors</strong> — a categorization no other tool on the market captures. I redesigned it ground-up in partnership with <strong>Psynet</strong>, the psychometrics house that built the engine underneath. Every screen was a conversation between design and psychometric rigor; nothing in the assessment could bend if the math didn't allow it.</p>
        <p>The new Taylor Assessment was <strong>press-released globally in January 2024</strong>. It's now used by over 50,000 leaders in Walking the Talk's database — and it's the single tool that defined the firm's post-redesign positioning.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Chapter 03 -->
    <article class="sp2-chapter">
      <div class="sp2-chapter-index">
        <span class="sp2-chapter-num">03</span>
        <span class="sp2-chapter-date">2023–2024 · Culture Insights Survey</span>
      </div>
      <div class="sp2-chapter-body">
        <h3>Weeks of waiting collapsed into minutes.</h3>
        <p>Culture Insights Survey measures <strong>ninety behaviors across multiple languages</strong> — a 15-to-20-minute assessment designed to produce immediate portal results. Before the redesign, HR teams waited days or weeks for a consultant to compile data and write it up. After the redesign, a CHRO could open the portal the moment the survey closed and walk into a board meeting with findings the same afternoon.</p>
        <p>The shift wasn't cosmetic. The entire service model moved from <em>consultant-delivered</em> to <em>self-serve-with-expert-support-when-needed</em>.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════
